# KISS — Keep It Simple, Stupid

All generated and edited code must be the **simplest solution that meets the current requirement**. Do not overengineer.

Simplicity does **not** override good programming practices: Clean Code, SOLID (applied to what exists now), the style guide, project architecture, security, validation, and tests.

Sequential, explicit code is **not** a license to add extra classes, helpers, config, or indirection. Named intermediate values are required; extra layers are not.

## Principle

If a straightforward implementation works, use it. Do not add generality, indirection, or structure for hypothetical future needs (YAGNI).

Prefer the language, standard library, and framework. Add a new abstraction only when the requirement needs it **now**, or the same logic already exists in several places.

> Any fool can write code that a computer can understand. Good programmers write code that humans can understand. — Robert C. Martin

## Clean Code

Write code people can read, debug, and change without decoding cleverness.

- Use names that say what the value or step is
- Keep functions and methods small and focused on one idea
- Prefer obvious sequential steps over nested or implicit flow
- Remove duplication that already exists; do not invent a shared layer for a single use
- Do not use comments to explain unclear code — make the code clear
- Handle errors and edge cases that the requirement needs; do not skip tests

## SOLID (at the scale of the current requirement)

Apply SOLID to the code you are writing now. Do not add types, interfaces, or extension points for a future that is not in the requirement.

- **Single Responsibility Principle** — a class or method has one reason to change. A complete business step is one reason; wrapping `trim` is not.
- **Open/Closed Principle** — extend existing seams when they already exist. Do not create new ones “so we can extend later”.
- **Liskov Substitution Principle** — subtypes must honor the contract of the type they replace. Do not inherit just to share a few lines.
- **Interface Segregation Principle** — depend only on methods you use. Do not add a wide interface for one caller.
- **Dependency Inversion Principle** — depend on an abstraction when there are real multiple implementations or a needed seam. Do not add an interface for a single hardcoded class.

## Do

- Solve only what was asked
- Use existing project patterns (thin controllers, services for business logic, Form Requests) instead of inventing new ones
- Inline one-off logic; extract a method or class only for a real business step or reused logic
- Hardcode values that will not change at runtime; change them in git
- Keep auth, authorization, validation, and other safety guards
- Follow `.cursor/rules/style-guide.md`

## Do not

- Add classes, interfaces, adapters, factories, pipelines, or config for a single hardcoded implementation
- Wrap a trivial operation in a helper
- Re-process data that the framework, validator, or schema already handled
- Use a more powerful tool (regex, parser, event, job, cache, queue) when a direct call is enough
- Design for reuse, extensibility, or “we might need it later” unless the requirement says so
- Skip tests, security, architecture, Clean Code, or SOLID in the name of simplicity

## Examples

```php
// BAD — extra type and helper for one use
interface PromptLoaderInterface {}
$prompt = $this->promptLoader->load(config('ai.prompt_path'));

// GOOD — current need is a file
$prompt = trim((string) File::get(resource_path('prompts/qualify.txt')));
```

```php
// BAD — strategy/factory for two branches
$handler = $this->handlerFactory->make($type);
$handler->handle($payload);

// GOOD
if ($type === 'email') {
    $this->sendEmail($payload);
} else {
    $this->sendWebhook($payload);
}
```

```php
// BAD — helper whose only job is a trivial wrap
$companyName = $this->normalizeCompanyName($name);

// GOOD
$companyName = strtolower(trim($name));
```

```php
//BAD overngineered
public function handle(array $context): array
{
    $opportunity = $this->loadOpportunity($context);
    $client = $this->loadClient($opportunity);

    // ...
}

private function loadOpportunity(array $context): Opportunity
{
    $rawOpportunityId = $context['opportunity_id'] ?? null;

    if ($rawOpportunityId === null) {
        throw new RuntimeException('Qualification requires an opportunity_id.');
    }

    $opportunityId = (int) $rawOpportunityId;
    $opportunity = Opportunity::find($opportunityId);

    if ($opportunity === null) {
        throw new RuntimeException('Qualification opportunity not found: '.$opportunityId);
    }

    return $opportunity;
}

private function loadClient(Opportunity $opportunity): Client
{
    $client = $opportunity->client;

    if ($client === null) {
        throw new RuntimeException('Qualification client not found for opportunity: '.$opportunity->id);
    }

    return $client;
}

//Good - KISS
$id = $context['opportunity_id'];
$oportunity = Opportunity::with(client)
                    ->findOrFail($id);
$client = $oportunity->client;
```